Recorded playback probe lifecycle
=================================

Recorded playback and codec discovery now share the bounded FFprobe runner in
TranscodeService. Recorded discovery retains its TCP transport, 2,000,000-byte
probe-size setting, 3,000,000-microsecond analysis setting and 10-second timeout.
Stdout is limited to 1 MiB; stderr is discarded. Cancellation, timeout and output
refusal kill and reap the owned child before returning or propagating cancellation.

The recorded result keeps its existing meaning:
- None: discovery was inconclusive; do not cache a definitive playback verdict.
- playable=False: a successful, valid probe contains no video or zero dimensions.
- playable=True: valid video metadata with positive dimensions.

A nonzero probe exit, malformed metadata, invalid dimensions or oversized output
cannot become a definitive cached result. H.264/HEVC normalization remains the
same, including HEVC -> h265. Missing probe binaries are inconclusive here.

Tests use real harmless child processes. Separate packaged-binary qualification
uses local synthetic H.264/HEVC files, not RTSP or vendor hardware. This change
does not validate every camera/NVR codec or claim production device acceptance.

Recorded-probe cache invalidation, HLS session-key scope/window completeness,
FFmpeg worker ownership and per-viewer leases remain separate lifecycle work.
